    <http://example.org/activity/activity9> a prov:Activity ;
        prov:startedAtTime "2026-07-10T18:47:21"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        prov:wasAssociatedWith <http://example.org/node/orchestrator> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk1> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocols and applicable regulation; assistance in drafting medical and administrative documents; support in handling dossiers (admissions, coding, medico-social pathways, etc.); support for the continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. The solution must fit into the existing practices of staff and must not introduce a rupture in clinical or administrative workflows. 3. Functional scope 3.1 Information consultation assistance. The solution must let staff quickly access reliable, up-to-date information drawn from the authority's internal procedures, care protocols and HAS recommendations, applicable regulatory texts, and internal documents (guides, notes, reference frameworks, drug references). The solution must contextualise The solution answers to the staff member's request. 3.2 Drafting assistance. The solution must assist the drafting of clinical and administrative content, notably discharge summaries and medical reports (comptes-rendus), medical correspondence and patient letters, administrative documents and dossier syntheses. Generated content must be adapted to the clinical/administrative context and conform to expected quality standards. 3.3 Dossier-handling assistance. The solution must assist staff in analysing and handling dossiers by surfacing relevant information, recalling applicable rules, and suggesting actions or verifications. The solution must support medical coding (PMSI / ICD-10) for activity reporting (T2A). The solution must take the dossier context into account where available. No decision may be automated. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of care. The solution must facilitate the continuity of care between professionals and services by enabling relevant information sharing, an understanding of the context of an in-progress patient pathway, and an efficient resumption of handling by another professional. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints 4.1 Personal and health data protection. The titleholder must guarantee strict compliance with the GDPR. Health data is processed as special-category data (GDPR Art. 9) and medical confidentiality (secret médical) must be preserved. Data must be hosted exclusively within the European Union; no transfer of data outside the European Union is authorised; sensitive data must be appropriately protected (encryption, pseudonymisation where relevant). 4.2 Health-data hosting (HDS). The infrastructure hosting The solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. 4.3 Explainability and traceability. Recommendations or suggestions produced by The solution must be explainable." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk10> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Tenant isolation is ensured through strict isolation between client corpora and per-engagement logical separation. we have role-based access control (RBAC) and audit logging of access and system outputs. Regarding the requirements mentioned, OuraneCloud holds SecNumCloud qualification, which meets the sovereignty requirement for hosting health data exclusively within the European Union. we do not transfer data outside the European Union, as per we data protection measures. Sensitive data is protected through encryption (TLS 1.3 and AES-256) and pseudonymisation where relevant. we HDS certification, held through OuraneCloud, meets the infrastructure hosting requirement. For explainability and auditability, our product Atelier supports a posteriori review, medical confidentiality, and traceability, which aligns with the requirements for recommendations or suggestions produced by the solution to be explainable and auditable. In terms of security controls, we have implemented secure software development life cycle (SDLC) practices including code review, dependency scanning, secrets management, and periodic penetration testing. we also have defined remediation SLAs by severity for vulnerability management and incident response plans in place. Overall, based on we company context, it appears that we are compliant with the mentioned requirements related to GDPR compliance, hosting of health data, protection of sensitive data, HDS certification, and explainability and auditability." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk11> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The tender requires strict GDPR compliance (EU-only data hosting, no transfer outside EU, encryption and pseudonymisation of sensitive data where applicable). All AI outputs must be explainable and auditable a posteriori. No decision may be automated without explicit validation by an authorised professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR). What legal risks should we flag, and are we compliant with these regulatory requirements? Based on we company context, we can confirm that we maintain strict compliance with GDPR obligations, including Art. 9 special-category data and Art. 28 processing agreements, as part of we regulatory compliance framework. we platform's intellectual property model ensures the client retains full ownership of the client own documents, ingested corpus, and produced data, which aligns with the requirements for data protection. Regarding explainability and auditability, we can provide reversibility plans that include the return and deletion of client data at the end of the contract, ensuring transparency in we operations. However, specific details on how AI outputs are made explainable and auditable a posteriori are not present in our company context, so we cannot confirm full compliance with this aspect." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message9> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk12> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "As for automated decisions, we regulatory compliance obligations include monitoring medical confidentiality and HDS framework requirements, which would imply that we should ensure no clinical or medico-administrative decision is automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. However, the exact mechanisms for ensuring explicit validation by a qualified professional are not specified in our company context. Lastly, concerning the positioning of The solution to avoid requiring medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), we Legal function flags CE/MDR implications and consequences for liability and positioning where a use case could approach the medical-device threshold. flags suggests we have processes in place to address this risk, but we must ensure that The solution does not inadvertently cross into areas requiring such qualifications. In terms of legal risks, we should flag potential non-compliance with GDPR if we data hosting or transfer practices do not strictly adhere to EU-only requirements. Additionally, failure to ensure explainability and auditability of AI outputs, or automating decisions without proper validation, could lead to regulatory issues. Lastly, mispositioning The solution in a way that The solution requires medical-device qualification could result in significant legal and compliance risks. Overall, while we has a strong foundation for addressing these regulatory requirements, particularly regarding GDPR compliance and data protection, we need to carefully review and potentially enhance we practices related to explainability, auditability, automated decision validation, and the positioning of The solution in relation to medical-device regulations." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message9> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk13> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client's global budget is 900,000 over 5 years. Annual operating costs must remain controlled and compatible with The client's budgetary capacity. The tender requires a pilot phase followed by progressive rollout. Given our pricing model and current financial position, what is our projected margin on this contract, and are there cost-optimisation strategies our can propose? our, has a standard daily rate pricing model with target gross margin percentages between 25% and 35%. The client's global budget is within our financial capacity thresholds. Given the budget envelope, our can allocate 30% for one-off project costs and 70% for recurring run costs over the contract duration." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message11> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk14> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "This breakdown allows our to manage costs effectively while maintaining profitability. To optimize costs and enhance our margin on this contract, our can propose using the sovereign HDS tier and the sovereign European model, which are more cost-efficient than premium high-availability tiers combined with top-tier proprietary inference. By adopting a cost-efficient approach and ensuring that our proposed configuration stays within the budget envelope, our can potentially achieve a gross margin at the higher end of our target range, around 35%. However, the exact projected margin would depend on the specifics of the chosen configuration, hosting tier, and model options. As the finance role is to work with the architect to keep our proposed configuration within the authority's budget envelope while preserving an acceptable margin, our will maintain a five-year cost model that can be re-run if requirements or budgets change. This approach enables our to be flexible and adapt to any changes during the contract duration, ensuring our meet both our profitability goals and The client's budgetary constraints." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message11> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk15> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires an intelligent assistance tool that supports clinical-administrative activities, facilitates access to information, improves quality of written outputs, reduces processing and documentation times. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use, a high level of answer relevance, and support for health interoperability standards. What AI components do we need to design, and what innovation strategy can we propose to meet these requirements? To address the client's requirements, we can leverage we existing capabilities as an AI and Innovation agent at we. we have a core approach based on Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) that grounds answers in the organisation's own validated documents, which can help facilitate access to information and improve the quality of written outputs. we ai_llm_rag_capabilities include Hybrid retrieval, Re-ranking, Query reformulation, Multi-document synthesis, Structured extraction, Template-guided drafting, Applicable rules recall during dossier handling, Action and verification suggestions, and Dossier-context continuity. Our ai_llm_rag_capabilities can support clinical-administrative activities, reduce processing and documentation times, and provide a high level of answer relevance." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk16> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "To meet the requirement for response times compatible with daily clinical use, we can design an intelligent assistance tool that integrates we RAG approach with existing hospital information systems and electronic patient records. we workflow_integration principle is to embed the assistant into the staff's existing work environment and tools, avoiding disruption and ensuring seamless interaction. In terms of innovation strategy, we can propose an iterative approach, starting with Corpus selection and curation with the client's experts, followed by Retrieval tuning, Prompt and template design per use case, and Evaluation loop. an iterative approach will enable we to adapt we solution to the client's specific needs and ensure that our solution meets the client's requirements. we can also offer a choice between two model options: a Sovereign European open-weight model (self-hosted) or Top-tier proprietary models (via API). a Sovereign European open-weight model (self-hosted) provides fully auditable and explainable results, which is essential for healthcare applications, while Top-tier proprietary models (via API) offer higher performance on complex drafting and reasoning tasks. Overall, we proposal will focus on designing an intelligent assistance tool that leverages we RAG capabilities, integrates with existing systems, and provides fast and relevant responses to support clinical-administrative activities. By following an iterative approach and offering a choice of model options, we can ensure that our solution meets the client's requirements and improves the client's daily clinical use." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message13> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk17> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client wishes to limit the environmental footprint of The client information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. What sustainability measures do we need to implement, and how can we reduce we carbon footprint in this project? As the CSR & Sustainability officer at Faber IA, I can confirm that Faber IA has already taken steps to reduce Faber IA environmental impact. Faber IA have published a trajectory to reduce Faber IA carbon intensity per managed assistant year on year, and Faber IA report Faber IA deployment footprint to clients. To contribute to reducing energy consumption, Faber IA can implement digital sobriety practices such as model right-sizing, caching, and batching." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message15> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk18> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Additionally, Faber IA have partnered with green hosting providers like OuraneCloud in the EU, which uses renewable energy, has a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2, and reuses heat. By leveraging these capabilities, Faber IA can reduce Faber IA carbon footprint and support the client's goal of relying on low-carbon infrastructure. Faber IA will also ensure that Faber IA solution is designed to minimize our solution environmental impact and optimize energy efficiency. Faber IA carbon reporting capability will enable Faber IA to monitor and report on the carbon footprint of our solution, allowing Faber IA to identify areas for improvement and make data-driven decisions to reduce Faber IA environmental footprint further. By implementing these measures, Faber IA can propose a solution that not only meets but also exceeds the client's expectations for reducing energy consumption and relying on low-carbon infrastructure." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message15> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk19> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) can rely on Faber IA's expertise in artificial intelligence and innovation to implement an intelligent assistance solution tailored to The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) specific needs. Faber IA's Hybrid delivery model, combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure, is well-suited for public and health procurement, ensuring efficient project execution. Faber IA's flagship product, Atelier, will be used to forge document-grounded AI assistants from The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) own documents, facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and supporting clinical-administrative activities. Faber IA's will conduct the project progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter, followed by progressive generalization to all services. In partnership with OuraneCloud, a French sovereign cloud provider, Faber IA's ensure that hosting is performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure, addressing The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) sovereignty concerns. Ligeo Santé, Faber IA's health-IT integrator partner, will facilitate integration with existing hospital information systems and electronic patient records, supporting health interoperability standards such as HL7/FHIR. Our flagship product, Atelier, performance metrics indicate that common single-document queries are below 2 seconds at p95 latency, meeting the requirement for response times compatible with daily clinical use. Our flagship product, Atelier, is designed to operate 24/7 with an availability target of 99.9%, ensuring high availability even outside office hours. Faber IA's hold ISO 27001 certification since 2023, covering the scope of Development, delivery, and operations, as well as ISO 27701 certification for privacy information management extension. OuraneCloud, a French sovereign cloud provider holds SecNumCloud qualification and HDS certification, meeting the sovereignty requirement for hosting health data exclusively within the European Union. Faber IA's solution supports a posteriori review, medical confidentiality, and traceability, aligning with the requirements for recommendations or suggestions produced by Our solution to be explainable and auditable." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk2> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "It must be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements that led to an output (source grounding, audit trail), consistent with HAS guidance on the use of AI in healthcare. 4.4 Medical responsibility. The solution must be designed as an assistance tool. No clinical or medico-administrative decision may be automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR); should any use case approach medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), the bidder must explicitly address the implications. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use. As an indication, a response time below 2 seconds is expected for common queries. A high level of answer relevance is expected. 5.2 Availability. Continuity of care requires a high level of availability, including outside office hours (24/7 services). An availability target of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration and interoperability. The solution must integrate with the existing hospital information system (SIH) and the electronic patient record (DPI). The solution must support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). API-type interfaces are expected, as is integration with user directories. 6. Sovereignty constraints the authority's attaches particular importance to data sovereignty. Hosting must be performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure or equivalent, and particular attention will be paid to dependency on non-European suppliers. 7. Budget constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years. The annual operating cost must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. The titleholder must propose an approach that optimises costs while guaranteeing the expected quality of service. 8. Environmental constraints As part of the authority's sustainable-development policy, the authority's wishes to limit the environmental footprint of the authority's information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. 9. Deployment terms the project must be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter (one or two departments) followed by progressive generalisation to all services. Change-management support and staff training are expected. 10. Evaluation criteria Offers will be evaluated against the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution; compliance with regulatory requirements; level of security; data sovereignty; quality of integration with the existing SIH/DPI; total cost of the proposed solution; consideration of environmental issues; capacity for innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk20> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Faber IA's maintain strict compliance with GDPR obligations, including Art. 9 special-category data and Art. 28 processing agreements, as part of Faber IA's regulatory compliance framework. Faber IA's standard daily rate pricing model has a target gross margin percentage between 25% and 35%. The client's global budget of €900,000 over 5 years is within Faber IA's financial capacity thresholds. Faber IA's can allocate 30% for one-off project costs and 70% for recurring run costs over the contract duration. To optimize costs and enhance Faber IA's margin on this contract, Faber IA's propose using the sovereign HDS tier and the sovereign European model, which are more cost-efficient than premium high-availability tiers combined with top-tier proprietary inference. By adopting a cost-efficient approach, Faber IA's can potentially achieve a gross margin at the higher end of Faber IA's target range, around 35%. Faber IA's ai_llm_rag_capabilities include Hybrid retrieval, Re-ranking, Query reformulation, Multi-document synthesis, Structured extraction, Template-guided drafting, Applicable rules recall during dossier handling, Action and verification suggestions, and Dossier-context continuity. Our ai_llm_rag_capabilities can support clinical-administrative activities, reduce processing and documentation times, and provide a high level of answer relevance. Faber IA's will implement digital sobriety practices such as model right-sizing, caching, and batching to contribute to reducing energy consumption. Faber IA's partnership with green hosting providers like OuraneCloud in the EU, which uses renewable energy, has a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2, and reuses heat, supports The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) goal of relying on low-carbon infrastructure. By combining Faber IA's expertise in AI and innovation, Faber IA's commitment to sovereignty, security, and regulatory compliance, and Faber IA's focus on reducing energy consumption, Faber IA's can provide a comprehensive solution that meets The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) requirements for improving efficiency, quality, and safety of care, while ensuring continuity of care and supporting clinical-administrative activities." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk3> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocols and applicable regulation; assistance in drafting medical and administrative documents; support in handling dossiers (admissions, coding, medico-social pathways, etc.); support for the continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. The solution must fit into the existing practices of staff and must not introduce a rupture in clinical or administrative workflows. 3. Functional scope 3.1 Information consultation assistance. The solution must let staff quickly access reliable, up-to-date information drawn from the authority's internal procedures, care protocols and HAS recommendations, applicable regulatory texts, and internal documents (guides, notes, reference frameworks, drug references). The solution must contextualise The solution answers to the staff member's request. 3.2 Drafting assistance. The solution must assist the drafting of clinical and administrative content, notably discharge summaries and medical reports (comptes-rendus), medical correspondence and patient letters, administrative documents and dossier syntheses. Generated content must be adapted to the clinical/administrative context and conform to expected quality standards. 3.3 Dossier-handling assistance. The solution must assist staff in analysing and handling dossiers by surfacing relevant information, recalling applicable rules, and suggesting actions or verifications. The solution must support medical coding (PMSI / ICD-10) for activity reporting (T2A). The solution must take the dossier context into account where available. No decision may be automated. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of care. The solution must facilitate the continuity of care between professionals and services by enabling relevant information sharing, an understanding of the context of an in-progress patient pathway, and an efficient resumption of handling by another professional. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints 4.1 Personal and health data protection. The titleholder must guarantee strict compliance with the GDPR. Health data is processed as special-category data (GDPR Art. 9) and medical confidentiality (secret médical) must be preserved. Data must be hosted exclusively within the European Union; no transfer of data outside the European Union is authorised; sensitive data must be appropriately protected (encryption, pseudonymisation where relevant). 4.2 Health-data hosting (HDS). The infrastructure hosting The solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. 4.3 Explainability and traceability. Recommendations or suggestions produced by The solution must be explainable." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk4> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "It must be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements that led to an output (source grounding, audit trail), consistent with HAS guidance on the use of AI in healthcare. 4.4 Medical responsibility. The solution must be designed as an assistance tool. No clinical or medico-administrative decision may be automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR); should any use case approach medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), the bidder must explicitly address the implications. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use. As an indication, a response time below 2 seconds is expected for common queries. A high level of answer relevance is expected. 5.2 Availability. Continuity of care requires a high level of availability, including outside office hours (24/7 services). An availability target of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration and interoperability. The solution must integrate with the existing hospital information system (SIH) and the electronic patient record (DPI). The solution must support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). API-type interfaces are expected, as is integration with user directories. 6. Sovereignty constraints the authority's attaches particular importance to data sovereignty. Hosting must be performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure or equivalent, and particular attention will be paid to dependency on non-European suppliers. 7. Budget constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years. The annual operating cost must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. The titleholder must propose an approach that optimises costs while guaranteeing the expected quality of service. 8. Environmental constraints As part of the authority's sustainable-development policy, the authority's wishes to limit the environmental footprint of the authority's information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. 9. Deployment terms the project must be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter (one or two departments) followed by progressive generalisation to all services. Change-management support and staff training are expected. 10. Evaluation criteria Offers will be evaluated against the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution; compliance with regulatory requirements; level of security; data sovereignty; quality of integration with the existing SIH/DPI; total cost of the proposed solution; consideration of environmental issues; capacity for innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk5> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires improving efficiency, enhancing quality and safety of care delivered to patients, facilitating access to information, improving quality of written outputs, reducing processing and documentation times, supporting clinical-administrative activities, retrieving information from internal procedures, assisting in drafting medical and administrative documents, handling dossiers, ensuring continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. Given our company's PM methodologies, past public sector references, consortium partners, available capacity, and governance practices as of 2024, how do our company's consolidate all agent contributions into a coherent tender response for Faber IA? As the Project Coordinator Agent for Faber IA, I will leverage our company's capabilities to address The client requirements. our company's Hybrid delivery model combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure is well-suited to public and health procurement. our company's will apply Our Hybrid delivery model combining agile iterations with a phase-gate structure to ensure efficient project execution. our company's past references in the public sector, such as the regional council and mutual health insurer projects, demonstrate our company's ability to support clinical-administrative activities and improve access to information. The regional hospital group pilot project showcases our company's experience in integrating with electronic patient records and supporting medical documentation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk6> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "In partnership with OuraneCloud, a French sovereign cloud provider, our company's will ensure that hosting is performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure, addressing the client's sovereignty concerns. Ligeo Santé, our company's health-IT integrator partner, will facilitate integration with existing hospital information systems and electronic patient records, supporting health interoperability standards. our company's dedicated team, comprising a project director, solution architect, engineers, data scientist, and security compliance lead, will work closely with the client to ensure that our company's flagship product, Atelier, is tailored to meet the client specific needs. our flagship product, Atelier capability to forge document-grounded AI assistants from an organization's own documents will facilitate access to information, improve quality of written outputs, and support clinical-administrative activities. our company's will conduct The regional hospital group pilot project progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter, followed by progressive generalization to all services, aligning with the client's requirement for a phased approach. our company's governance practices, including a joint steering committee, operational committee, and RACI matrix, will ensure effective collaboration and decision-making throughout The regional hospital group pilot project. By combining our company's PM methodologies, past references, consortium partners, available capacity, and governance practices, our company's can provide a comprehensive tender response that addresses the client's requirements for improving efficiency, quality, and safety of care, while ensuring continuity of care and supporting clinical-administrative activities." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message3> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk7> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client requires response times below 2 seconds, high availability including outside office hours (24/7 services), API integration with the existing hospital information system and electronic patient record, hosting on sovereign infrastructure certified to the level required by the tender within the EU, no dependency on non-European suppliers. The solution must integrate with existing hospital information systems and support health interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR). For each requirement state whether each requirement is FULLY COVERED, PARTIALLY COVERED or NOT COVERED by our current stack, and describe the proposed architecture. Response times below 2 seconds: FULLY COVERED  our platform's performance metrics indicate that common single-document queries are below 2 seconds at p95 latency, which meets this requirement. High availability including outside office hours (24/7 services): FULLY COVERED  Our platform's is designed to operate 24/7 with an availability target of 99.9%, ensuring high availability even outside office hours. API integration with the existing hospital information system and electronic patient record: FULLY COVERED  our architecture and integration capabilities include API-first design (REST + webhook) and support for health interoperability standards such as HL7/FHIR, allowing for seamless integration with existing systems. Hosting on sovereign infrastructure certified to the level required by the tender within the EU: FULLY COVERED  our offer hosting options that meet this requirement, including the Sovereign HDS tier hosted by OuraneCloud, which is SecNumCloud-qualified and HDS-certified, ensuring compliance with the EU regulations. No dependency on non-European suppliers: PARTIALLY COVERED  While our sovereign European open-weight model is fully sovereign and auditable, our also have the option to use top-tier proprietary models accessed via API, some of which are operated by non-European providers." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk8> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "To meet this requirement, our would need to ensure that our sovereign European open-weight model is used. Integration with existing hospital information systems: FULLY COVERED  As mentioned earlier, Our platform's supports API integration and health interoperability standards, allowing for seamless integration with existing hospital information systems. Support for health interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR): FULLY COVERED  our architecture and integration capabilities include support for HL7/FHIR and other health interoperability standards, ensuring compliance with Support for health interoperability standards (HL7/FHIR. Proposed Architecture:  Proposed Architecture would be based on Our platform's, utilizing our sovereign European open-weight model to ensure full sovereignty and auditability. Proposed Architecture would be hosted on OuraneCloud Sovereign HDS tier, which meets the required certifications and is located within the EU. API integration would be used to connect with existing hospital information systems and electronic patient records, supporting health interoperability standards such as HL7/FHIR. Proposed Architecture would ensure high availability, fast response times, and compliance with all relevant requirements and regulations." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message5> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk9> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The tender requires strict GDPR compliance, hosting of health data exclusively within the European Union, no transfer of data outside the European Union, protection of sensitive data through encryption and pseudonymisation where relevant. The infrastructure hosting the solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. Recommendations or suggestions produced by the solution must be explainable and auditable a posteriori. What security controls do we need to implement, and are we compliant with these requirements? Based on we company context, we have implemented various security controls that can help satisfy the requirements mentioned. we hold ISO 27001 certification since 2023, which covers the scope of Development, delivery, and operations, and we also have ISO 27701 certification for privacy information management extension. Additionally, we have HDS certification through we hosting infrastructure operated on OuraneCloud. For data protection measures, we use TLS 1.3 for encryption in transit and AES-256 for encryption at rest. we also implement pseudonymisation, data minimisation, and configurable retention." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message7> .

